              TARGA RESOURCES CORP. POST-HEARING REPLY BRIEF

       Pursuant to the Hearings Examiners’ Order on Post-hearing briefing issued July 28, 2026,

Targa Resources Corp. (“Targa”) respectfully submits this Post-Hearing Reply Brief in support of

the Comprehensive Stipulation of parties filed on June 22, 2026 (“Stipulation”) in this case and

heard by the Hearings Examiners at the hearing on the Stipulation July 13 – 16, 2026. In support

thereof, and in reply to the briefs filed by other parties, Targa respectfully asserts the following:

       1.     Targa participated in settlement negotiations among the parties in this case. Targa

found the negotiations to be conducted in good faith and were very productive, leading to a fair

resolution among joining parties and a sound recommendation for the Commission.

       2.     Targa agrees with the parties filing briefs in this case that the Stipulation (a) is fully

supported by the evidence in the record; (b) is the outcome of serious bargaining among

knowledgeable parties, including those representing customers and the public as well as the utility

having the burden of proof in this case; (c) is consistent with well-established regulatory principles;



                                                  1
 (d) complies with New Mexico law; (e) provides a reasonable resolution of contested issues among

the parties; and (f) will result in fair, just, and reasonable rates.

        3.      The agreed revenue requirement and related capital structure and rate of return on

investment will enable SPS to provide safe and reliable service to customers, while also offering

reasonable and meaningful customer protections and other benefits.                 SPS made significant

movement from its filed case, and stipulating parties achieved meaningful modifications in cost

allocation that benefit customers, including that residential customers bearing proportionally a

lower amount of the rate increases than that proposed by SPS or Commission Staff.

        4.      The Stipulation meets and exceeds the Commission’s standards for approval of such

litigation settlement, including (a) resulting from serious bargaining among capable,

knowledgeable parties, (b) viewed as whole, benefits customers and the public interest, and (c)

viewed as a whole, does not violate any important regulatory principle or practice.1

        5.      The public interest was well represented in the Stipulation during intense

negotiations.    The Stipulation is supported by (a) the New Mexico Department of Justice

(“NMDOJ”) whose participation in this case is to “represent the interests of residential and small

business ratepayers otherwise unrepresented because, in its judgment, it is appropriate and the

public interest so requires . . . .”;2 (b) the Coalition for Clean Affordable Energy (“CCAE”) which

is made up of several organizations representing over 20,000 members participating in this case in

keeping with its mission to “promote clean and affordable energy policies in New Mexico. . . . .”;3




