 and (c) Prosperity Works which is “a non-profit grassroots organization that . . . advocates for

policies that generate economic prosperity for all New Mexicans . . . .”4

        6.     The Stipulation consists of carefully negotiated terms wherein parties made

compromises in order to achieve outcomes that best meet their respective interests. The terms

cannot be viewed as severable; any material changes would destroy the delicate bargain reached

by the parties and would negate the Stipulation. For approval, the standard is for the Commission

to determine whether the resulting rates fairly balance customer and investor interests and fall

within the zone of reasonableness.5

        7.     The Stipulation contains meaningful customer protections that were advocated by

various parties that enhance the benefits to the public, including greater credit to New Mexico

customers for the value of resources assigned to them, Southwest Power Pool deficiency payment

risk guardrails, and reduction to SPS returns related to Production Tax Credits and Deferred Tax

Asset structures (all briefed in more detail by other parties).            SPS’s requests for revenue

requirement were reduced by about fifty percent, and their requested rate of return on equity and

related mechanics of cost recovery structure were substantially moderated.

        8.     Importantly, the Stipulation provides useful transparency for the Commission and

the public to see. There is no “black box” regarding revenue requirements and cost-of-service

adjustments. SPS’s witnesses provided detailed, updated information so that the Commission and

the parties can verify all of the adjustments made to conform to the Stipulation.6

        9.     Customer growth in the SPS service area SPS has dramatically increased and will

continue to grow, driven primarily by rapid economic development in the region. Yet several of



